<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#onojie">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Onojie</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Enijie</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Onojie, plural Enijie, is the title of the hereditary traditional rulers of the Esan chiefdoms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-state">Edo State</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. Tradition traces many Enijie to princes and nobles from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Kingdom of Benin</a>, and they owed allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> of Benin while governing their own chiefdoms with chiefs and age grades. Each chiefdom, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uromi">Uromi</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekpoma">Ekpoma</a>, still has its Onojie.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/onojie.jpg" alt="Onojie" width="320" height="520" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ehizibue efua20 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ogbidi_Okojie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
